// Exercise 4
//
// Explanation:
// K's lexer/scanner (generated by flex) tokenizes the input in a separate
// phase *before* parsing, using maximal munch: at each position it always
// picks the *longest* token that matches one of the grammar's terminals.
// The terminals here are "a", "abb", and "b". When scanning the input
// "abb" starting at position 0, the terminal "abb" matches all 3
// characters, while "a" only matches 1 character. Maximal munch always
// prefers the longer match, so the scanner unconditionally produces a
// single token `abb`, never the three tokens `a`, `b`, `b`.
//
// Because tokenization happens before parsing and is deterministic, the
// parser only ever sees ONE possible token stream for the input "abb":
// the single token `abb`. It therefore only ever finds the single parse
// `syntax Expr ::= "abb"`, and the alternative parse via
// `"a" Expr "b"` (with the inner `Expr` matching the token `b`) is never
// even considered, since it would require a *different* tokenization of
// the same input. With only one token stream, there is only one possible
// parse, so the parser correctly reports no ambiguity.
//
// Fix: remove the "abb" keyword terminal and instead spell it out using
// the "a" and "b" terminals that are already used elsewhere in the
// grammar. This removes the longer terminal that was winning maximal
// munch, so "abb" now scans as three separate tokens `a`, `b`, `b` --
// exactly the tokens needed by both candidate parses -- and the genuine
// grammar-level ambiguity becomes visible to the parser.
module EXERCISE4

  syntax Expr ::= "a" Expr "b"
                | "a" "b" "b"
                | "b"

endmodule
